# Phase 006 Group 04 — normative Assessment guardrails

Status: **Accepted — HLTH-030–HLTH-040; H04-01–H04-40 pass. Group 05 — Transformation Reconciliation & Metric Propagation is next and has not started.**

Preserve permanently:

- Expectation/criterion ≠ Observation evidence ≠ Baseline comparison ≠ normative Assessment outcome ≠ warning/proximity ≠ severity/priority ≠ waiver/response disposition ≠ composite health.
- Bind every criterion to exact subject/dimension, metric/check/structural definition, grain/population/window/context, operator/direction, inclusive/exclusive boundary, unit/denominator and required reference basis.
- Vendor/display shorthand never invents units, denominator, inclusivity, tolerance or hysteresis.
- `<=2%` and `<2%` are different normative criteria.
- Warning/proximity can coexist with `meets`; warning is not violation or low severity.
- A violated criterion stays `violates` regardless of low severity or alert treatment.
- Relative criteria must bind their reference identity/version and formula explicitly.
- Baseline remains descriptive even when an Expectation explicitly adopts `within X% of Baseline B`.
- Non-comparable/ambiguous/insufficient/unavailable required reference can make that relative criterion indeterminate; do not substitute another Baseline silently.
- Authoritative criterion + unsuitable evidence ≠ pass/fail.
- Missing telemetry ≠ violation; sufficient absence/opportunity coverage can support violation when occurrence is required.
- Approximate evidence can support `meets`/`violates` only when material uncertainty cannot change the boundary side or an explicit valid uncertainty treatment applies.
- Preserve at least `meets`, `violates`, `indeterminate/insufficient evidence`, `conflicting`, `unavailable`, and `not applicable` for one bound normative criterion.
- `Atypical` is Baseline-relative; it is not a normative result.
- Typical + violates, atypical + meets, typical + meets, and atypical + violates are all valid evidence-supported combinations.
- A new/post-change regime may have no Baseline yet while an independent explicit Expectation is immediately evaluable.
- Distinct-dimension/context/consumer Expectations can coexist; same-proposition incompatible co-authoritative rules remain conflict absent explicit resolution/composition.
- Never use strictest, loosest, newest, business, technical, highest-severity or numerically closest hidden precedence.
- `violates + waived response` ≠ `meets`.
- A bounded exception that makes a criterion non-applicable is distinct from a waiver that changes only response/consequence.
- Waiver scope does not propagate automatically from alerting to gate/control or another consequence class.
- Severity/priority/escalation does not decide criterion outcome; criticality does not automatically tighten thresholds or prove Impact.
- Historical Assessments retain exact criterion, warning/tolerance, waiver, evidence and referenced Baseline versions.
- Later corrected evidence creates reassessment; do not silently mutate historical Assessment.
- Group 05 must not propagate local warning/violation/severity/waiver statuses through Lineage; derive transformation-specific reconciliation propositions instead.

Do not select DQX/Metric Views expression syntax, alert engines, statistical libraries, storage, compute or service architecture from these rules.

Do not begin Group 05 or a later Phase 006 group without explicit user direction.
